<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The brand mark, drawing itself. Used once, by MainActivity's splash-exit listener.

  The trick here is that this is not a stroked *approximation* of ic_app_logo — it is the
  same shape. That logo is a filled polygon: a 12-wide stem at x 34..46 running y 30..82,
  and a 12-tall foot at y 70..82 running x 34..66. A 12-wide stroke down the stem's
  centreline (x=40) and along the foot's centreline (y=76), with BUTT caps and a MITER join,
  covers exactly that union — the caps keep the two free ends flat and the miter fills the
  outer corner at (34,82). So when trimPathEnd reaches 1 the traced line *is* the logo, and
  no cross-fade into a second drawable is needed.

  Change the stroke width, the caps or the join and that identity breaks silently: the trace
  will still look fine on its own but will no longer land on the mark it shares a screen with.

  Drawn WHITE and tinted at the point of use with app:tint="?attr/lpAccent", the same
  contract as ic_app_logo / lp_hero_glow / lp_dot — one drawable for all eight palettes, and
  no reliance on theme-attribute resolution inside an <animated-vector>, which is the one
  place it is genuinely unreliable.
-->
<animated-vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt">

    <aapt:attr name="android:drawable">
        <vector
            android:width="108dp"
            android:height="108dp"
            android:viewportWidth="108"
            android:viewportHeight="108">
            <path
                android:name="mark"
                android:pathData="M40,30 V76 H66"
                android:strokeColor="#FFFFFF"
                android:strokeLineCap="butt"
                android:strokeLineJoin="miter"
                android:strokeWidth="12"
                android:trimPathEnd="0" />
        </vector>
    </aapt:attr>

    <!--
      One confident stroke. fast_out_slow_in rather than a plain decelerate: the pen should
      commit and then arrive, and a trace is the one case where arriving and stopping is
      correct rather than abrupt — it is a line finishing, not an element settling.

      Path length is 72 units (46 down, 26 across), so the stem takes ~64% of the duration.
      That is uniform pen speed, which is what reads as handwriting.

      The duration is mirrored by MainActivity.TRACE_MS, because srcCompat gives no
      completion callback for an <animated-vector> and the bloom that follows is timed
      against this number. Change one and you must change the other.
    -->
    <target android:name="mark">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:duration="400"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/fast_out_slow_in"
                android:propertyName="trimPathEnd"
                android:valueFrom="0"
                android:valueTo="1"
                android:valueType="floatType" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>

</animated-vector>
